     "t": "code",
     "lang": "python",
     "code": "\"\"\"\nFRAME OBJECTS (PyFrameObject)\n==============================\n\nA frame object represents an execution context — one per function call.\nFrames form a call stack (linked list via f_back).\n\nKey Attributes:\n  f_back      → previous frame (caller)\n  f_code      → code object being executed\n  f_locals    → local variables (dict)\n  f_globals   → global variables (dict)\n  f_builtins  → built-in namespace (dict)\n  f_lineno    → current line number\n  f_lasti     → index of last bytecode instruction executed\n  f_trace     → trace function (for debuggers)\n\nFrame Lifecycle:\n  1. Function called → new frame created\n  2. Frame pushed onto call stack\n  3. Bytecodes executed using frame's context\n  4. Function returns → frame popped from stack\n  5. Frame deallocated (or cached in free list)\n\nAccessing frames:\n  - sys._getframe() → current frame\n  - inspect.currentframe() → current frame\n  - traceback objects have frame references\n\nPerformance note:\n  - Frame creation is a significant overhead for function calls\n  - CPython caches up to 200 frames in a free list\n  - Python 3.11+: frames are allocated inline on the C stack (faster)\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef demo_frame_objects():\n    \"\"\"Inspect frame objects.\"\"\"\n    def inner():\n        frame = sys._getframe()  # Current frame\n        print(f\"  Current function: {frame.f_code.co_name}\")\n        print(f\"  Caller function: {frame.f_back.f_code.co_name}\")\n        print(f\"  Line number: {frame.f_lineno}\")\n        print(f\"  Local vars: {list(frame.f_locals.keys())}\")\n        return frame\n\n    def outer():\n        x = 42\n        result = inner()\n        return result\n\n    print(\"  Frame inspection:\")\n    outer()\n\n    # Call stack depth\n    print(f\"\\n  Current recursion limit: {sys.getrecursionlimit()}\")\n\n\nprint(\"Q133: Frame Objects\")\ndemo_frame_objects()\nprint()",
