     "t": "code",
     "lang": "python",
     "code": "\"\"\"\nHOW type() WORKS\n==================\n\ntype() has two forms:\n\n1. type(obj) → returns the type/class of obj\n   - Equivalent to obj.__class__\n   - Returns the type object\n   - Slightly different from isinstance() for inheritance\n\n2. type(name, bases, dict) → creates a NEW class dynamically\n   - name: class name (string)\n   - bases: tuple of base classes\n   - dict: class namespace (methods, attributes)\n   - This is how Python creates classes internally!\n\nInternally:\n  - Every object has an ob_type pointer to its type object\n  - type() just reads this pointer\n  - O(1) operation\n\nThe type hierarchy:\n  type(42)          → <class 'int'>\n  type(int)         → <class 'type'>\n  type(type)        → <class 'type'>  (type is its own type!)\n\n  isinstance(42, int)     → True\n  isinstance(int, type)   → True\n  isinstance(type, type)  → True  (metaclass)\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef demo_type():\n    \"\"\"Show type() behavior.\"\"\"\n    # type() with one argument\n    objects = [42, 3.14, \"hello\", [1, 2], {\"a\": 1}, (1, 2), {1, 2}, None, True]\n    for obj in objects:\n        print(f\"  type({str(obj):12s}) = {type(obj).__name__}\")\n\n    # Type hierarchy\n    print(f\"\\n  type(42) = {type(42)}\")\n    print(f\"  type(int) = {type(int)}\")\n    print(f\"  type(type) = {type(type)}\")\n\n    # type() with three arguments — dynamic class creation\n    Dog = type(\"Dog\", (object,), {\n        \"species\": \"Canis familiaris\",\n        \"__init__\": lambda self, name: setattr(self, \"name\", name),\n        \"bark\": lambda self: f\"{self.name} says Woof!\",\n    })\n\n    rex = Dog(\"Rex\")\n    print(f\"\\n  Dynamic class: {rex.bark()}\")\n    print(f\"  type(rex) = {type(rex)}\")\n    print(f\"  Dog.__name__ = {Dog.__name__}\")\n    print(f\"  Dog.__bases__ = {Dog.__bases__}\")\n\n\nprint(\"Q136: How type() Works\")\ndemo_type()\nprint()",
